{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicConformalAngle","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.HyperbolicConformalAngle","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Geometry.Euclidean.Angle.Unoriented.Basic"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hypAngle_height_independent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hypAngle_height_independent","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicConformalAngle.lean#L80-L86","doc":"**Height-independence, stated on its own** (immediate from\n`hypAngle_eq_angle`): the hyperbolic angle at any two positive heights agrees.\nThis is what lets a dihedral angle along a vertical edge be measured at any\nconvenient height. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hypAngle_eq_angle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hypAngle_eq_angle","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicConformalAngle.lean#L58-L78","doc":"**CONFORMALITY (the linchpin).**  For every `z > 0`, the hyperbolic angle at\nheight `z` equals the Euclidean angle between the same tangent vectors — the\n`z⁻²` conformal factor cancels between numerator and denominator.\n\nIn particular the right-hand side does not mention `z`, so the hyperbolic angle\nis **height-independent**: the same two tangent directions subtend the same\nhyperbolic angle at every height. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hypAngle","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hypAngle","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/HyperbolicConformalAngle.lean#L50-L56","doc":"**The hyperbolic angle between tangent vectors `u, v` at height `z`** in the\nupper half-space model.  The conformal factor `z⁻²` multiplies the Euclidean\ninner product; the induced norms carry the same factor under a square root. ","signatureChunk":0}]}